//
// Expected: a1 — an attack that exists only because of a declared assumption
// has to name that assumption in its trace.
//
// Bob asserts `tag` against a MAC he recomputes. `kk` never leaks, so without
// the annotation there is no attack at all: cap_forgeable_names_its_assumption
// _absent.vp is that model and holds. With `forgeable`, the attacker produces
// MAC(kk, nil) without the key, replaces both `m` and `tag`, and Bob accepts.
//
// The trace used to explain this as "Attacker replaced m with nil, after which
// tag resolved to MAC(kk, nil)" -- reading the forged tag off the wire as if
// Alice had sent it, and never mentioning the assumption the whole attack
// rests on. Two things were wrong: the deduction rule consulted a narrower
// capability lookup than the validator did, so the forging step was never
// recorded as one; and absorption kept whichever explanation arrived first.
//
// It also used to print "Attacker constructs ASSERT(...)", because ASSERT was
// the one core primitive reconstruction did not refuse. ASSERT denotes a check,
// not a value: there is nothing there for an attacker to hold.
attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private kk
	knows public junk
	generates m
	tag = MAC[forgeable](kk, m)
]

Alice -> Bob: m, tag

principal Bob[
	knows private kk
	_ = ASSERT(tag, MAC(kk, m))?
	generates s
	ack = HASH(s, m)
]

Bob -> Alice: ack

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: tag
]
